---
.title = "Interfaces, inheritance, enums, traits and attributes",
.date = .date("2026-09-16T00:00:00"),
.layout = "doc.shtml",
.translation_key = "reference/registration/declarations",
.custom = { "section": "Declarations and registration" },
---

Declare inheritance, interfaces, enums, attributes and traits in the stub. For a declaration with no Zig instance data and no concrete methods to bind, register `phpz.ClassDecl("MyPHPExt\\Name", .{})`. For constructors or other method implementations, use `phpz.Class`. List the wrapper in `module.classes`. See [Classes, properties and methods]($link.page('reference/registration/classes')).

# [Interfaces]($heading.id('interfaces'))

```php
<?php
namespace MyPHPExt;

interface Identifiable {
    public function getId(): int;
}
```

```zig
pub const IdentifiableClass = phpz.ClassDecl("MyPHPExt\\Identifiable", .{});
```

An interface declares methods. Use `ClassDecl` when it needs no Zig handlers. Register parents and interfaces before classes that depend on them.

# [Registering parent classes and interfaces]($heading.id('inheritance'))

The following declarations come after the `Identifiable` interface. `Entity` is an abstract base class. `User` implements the interface and stores state in PHP properties:

```php
abstract class Entity implements Identifiable {
    public int $id = 0;
}

class User extends Entity {
    public function getId(): int {}
}
```

The generated registration function needs class entries for the parent and interfaces. The `.register` adapter takes a tuple of `*phpz.ClassEntry` arguments. Pass the wrappers' `.entry` values in the order specified by `register_class_*` in `_arginfo.h`:

```zig
fn registerEntity(register_fn: anytype) *phpz.ClassEntry {
    return register_fn(.{IdentifiableClass.entry});
}
const EntityClass = phpz.ClassDecl("MyPHPExt\\Entity", .{
    .register = registerEntity,
});

const User = struct {
    pub fn getId(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !void {
        try ctx.call.expectNoArgs();
        var scratch = phpz.Zval.raw.undef;
        defer phpz.Zval.raw.release(&scratch);
        const id = try ctx.call.this().?.property("id", false, &scratch);
        ctx.ret(.int, try id.as(.int));
    }
};

fn registerUser(register_fn: anytype) *phpz.ClassEntry {
    return register_fn(.{EntityClass.entry});
}
const UserClass = phpz.Class("MyPHPExt\\User", User, .{
    .register = registerUser,
});

comptime {
    const extension = @import("extension_info");
    phpz.module(.{
        .name = extension.name,
        .version = extension.version,
        .classes = &.{ IdentifiableClass, EntityClass, UserClass },
    });
}
```

Here `.classes` registers the interface, the parent and finally the subclass. `Entity` has no concrete methods and can use `ClassDecl`. `User::getId` needs a Zig implementation. After loading the extension:

```php
$user = new MyPHPExt\User();
$user->id = 42;
echo $user->getId(), PHP_EOL; // 42
var_dump($user instanceof MyPHPExt\Identifiable); // bool(true)
```

This example uses the standard object layout. For Zig-backed classes, `Class.fromObject()` checks both ancestry and object handlers before allowing access to a parent's Zig data. A backed class cannot extend a parent that allocates its own objects, including `Exception`, `ArrayObject` and other backed classes. For these subclasses, use `void` and keep state in PHP properties. Debug builds reject an unsupported parent with `error.UnsupportedParent`.

# [Enums]($heading.id('enums'))

```php
<?php
namespace MyPHPExt;

enum Status: int {
    case Inactive = 0;
    case Active = 1;
}
```

```zig
pub const StatusClass = phpz.ClassDecl("MyPHPExt\\Status", .{});
```

After registration, look up an enum case:

```zig
const case = StatusClass.entry.getEnumCase("Active");
```

`getEnumCase()` returns an optional object pointer. It returns `null` for a non-enum class, an unknown name or an ordinary class constant. For a known class and case, `getEnumCaseUnchecked()` skips validation and returns the pointer directly. Invalid input fails an assertion in Debug and may dereference a null constant in release builds. `enumCaseName()` returns the case name. For a backed enum, `enumCaseValue()` borrows the scalar zval. Unit enums have no backing value. The PHP enum runtime owns the case object. Follow the object ownership rules before returning it to PHP.

# [Traits]($heading.id('traits'))

```php
<?php
namespace MyPHPExt;

trait Greets {
    public function greet(): string {}
}
```

Implement concrete trait methods with matching `pub fn` declarations:

```zig
const Greets = struct {
    pub fn greet() []const u8 {
        return "Hello";
    }
};

const GreetsTrait = phpz.Class("MyPHPExt\\Greets", Greets, .{});
```

A trait with only abstract methods or no methods can use `ClassDecl`. The concrete `greet` above needs a Zig binding. Add `GreetsTrait` to `module.classes` so PHP classes can import it with `use MyPHPExt\Greets;`.

Using a trait adds no Zig instance data to the consuming class. Use a fieldless Zig container as above. A trait method can retrieve the current object through `Ctx` to access its PHP properties.

# [Attributes]($heading.id('attributes'))

```php
<?php
namespace MyPHPExt;
require "Zend/zend_attributes.stub.php";

#[\Attribute(\Attribute::TARGET_CLASS | \Attribute::TARGET_PARAMETER | \Attribute::IS_REPEATABLE)]
final class Tag {
    public string $name;
    public ?string $description;

    public function __construct(string $name, ?string $description = null) {}
}
```

`Tag` declares a concrete constructor and needs a `Class` binding even if the constructor body is empty:

```zig
const Tag = struct {
    pub fn __construct(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !void {
        const name, const description = try ctx.call.expectArgs(&.{
            .{ .string = .{} },
            .{ .string = .{ .optional = true, .nullable = true } },
        }, {});

        const tag = ctx.call.this().?;
        try tag.setProperty(.string, "name", name);

        if (description) |provided| {
            switch (provided) {
                .null => try tag.setProperty(.null, "description", {}),
                .value => |value| try tag.setProperty(.string, "description", value),
            }
        } else {
            try tag.setProperty(.null, "description", {});
        }
    }
};

const TagClass = phpz.Class("MyPHPExt\\Tag", Tag, .{});
```

For a marker attribute with no constructor or other concrete methods, use `ClassDecl`. For example, in the same namespace:

```php
#[\Attribute(\Attribute::TARGET_CLASS)]
final class Marker {}
```

```zig
const MarkerClass = phpz.ClassDecl("MyPHPExt\\Marker", .{});
```

Add `TagClass` and `MarkerClass` to `module.classes` to use the attributes from PHP:

```php
#[MyPHPExt\Tag("model")]
#[MyPHPExt\Marker]
class Record {}

$attribute = (new ReflectionClass(Record::class))
    ->getAttributes(MyPHPExt\Tag::class)[0];
echo $attribute->newInstance()->name, PHP_EOL; // model
```

The stub needs `require "Zend/zend_attributes.stub.php";` so the generator can resolve built-in attribute constants. The extension header also needs the C attribute declarations before including arginfo:

```c
#include "phpz.h"
#include "Zend/zend_attributes.h"
#include "my_php_extension_arginfo.h"
```

If `zig build gen-stub` cannot find `Zend/zend_attributes.stub.php`, copy it from the target PHP source into the extension's `Zend/` directory. Alternatively, use the generator from a complete php-src tree so it can resolve its own dependencies:

```sh
php /path/to/php-src/build/gen_stub.php my_php_extension.stub.php
```

Applying `#[Tag(...)]` to a class or parameter records metadata. The constructor runs when reflection instantiates the attribute.
